monitor them, understand them, and react to them when they do approach and then deal with the aftermath once they've left. Eric J. Dolan is the author of A Furious Sky, the 500-year history of America's hurricanes. For a lot of that history, Hurricanes were almost mystical events. The hand of God made manifest. Modern forecasting didn't exist until the mid-19th century. Before that, the hand of God made manifest. Modern forecasting didn't exist until the mid 19th century. Before that, the Taino, who were among the original inhabitants of the Caribbean, taught colonizers how to tell one was coming.
监测它们、理解它们，并在它们逼近时做出反应，然后在它们离开后处理善后工作。埃里克·J·多兰是《汹涌的天空：美国飓风五百年史》一书的作者。在很长一段时间里，飓风几乎是神秘莫测的事件。上帝之手的具象化。现代预报直到 19 世纪中叶才出现。在此之前，上帝之手的具象化。现代预报直到 19 世纪中叶才出现。在此之前，作为加勒比地区最早居民之一的泰诺人，教导殖民者如何辨别飓风的到来。

15
00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:31.000
There were small advances in understanding the basics of how they worked, but it wasn't until the 1870s that observers began to accurately predict where that hurricane was coming from and going to and how destructive it might be when it got there. That big leap forward came from a Jesuit working out of a coastal observatory in Havana, Cuba. His name was Father Benito Víñez, but he was also known as the Hurricane Priest. The observatory had decades worth of observations. So he read through them, collected them, systematized them.
在理解其运作原理的基本方面曾取得过一些小进展，但直到 19 世纪 70 年代，观察员们才开始准确预测飓风从何而来、去往何处，以及到达时可能会具有多大的破坏力。这一巨大飞跃源于一位在古巴哈瓦那海岸天文台工作的耶稣会士。他的名字叫本尼托·比涅斯神父，但他也被称为飓风神父。该天文台拥有数十年的观测资料。于是他研读了这些资料，将其收集并系统化。

16
00:07:31.000 --> 00:08:00.000
And he had plenty of equipment, even some he invented himself. He would take up to 10 measurements a day, different kinds of measurements, barometric measurements, precipitation, temperature. And he was really into watching clouds. Those were the wispy cirrostratus clouds. He called them cat's tails, coxplumes, wind feathers. And these clouds were followed by more denser clouds, cumulostratus and then cumulonimbus.
而且他有充足的设备，甚至有些是他自己发明的。他每天会进行多达 10 次测量，各种不同类型的测量，气压测量、降水、温度。他非常热衷于观察云层。那些是纤细的卷层云。他称它们为猫尾、公鸡羽毛、风之羽。这些云之后会跟着更密集的云，层积云，然后是积雨云。

17
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:51.000
These were all clues that he could use to predict where bad weather was coming from and headed to. So he was able to develop a very intimate understanding of hurricanes and how they behaved. And he put together a fairly impressive record of predictions that was more than just chance. Father Vignes set up a telegraph network for hurricane news from across the Caribbean. He made forecasts for the entire region that were printed in newspapers and eagerly read. It blew people's minds that someone could forecast the path of these storms with such precision. The hurricane priest didn't always get it right. Still, he and his Cuban colleagues developed an international reputation with their hurricane
这些都是他用来预测恶劣天气从何而来、去往何处的线索。因此，他能够对飓风及其行为方式产生非常深刻的理解。他整理了一份相当令人印象深刻的预测记录，这不仅仅是靠运气。比涅斯神父建立了一个电报网络，用于收集来自整个加勒比地区的飓风新闻。他为整个地区制作了预报，这些预报被印在报纸上并受到热切阅读。有人能以如此高的精度预测这些风暴的路径，这让人们大开眼界。飓风神父并非总能预测正确。尽管如此，他和他的古巴同事凭借飓风警告在国际上树立了声誉。

18
00:08:51.000 --> 00:09:18.000
warnings. Even after Father Vignes died in 1893, Cuba's forecasts were more accurate compared to the ones from other countries. In contrast, the U.S. system was sluggish and conservative. Meteorologists around the country had to wire their reports to Washington, D.C., where the central office would decide what was real and worth sharing. Warnings went out slowly, if at all. So the public was frequently caught off guard when big storms arrived.
甚至在比涅斯神父于 1893 年去世后，古巴的预报与来自其他国家的预报相比也更加准确。相比之下，美国的系统则迟缓而保守。全国各地的气象学家不得不将他们的报告拍电报发往华盛顿特区，由中央办公室决定什么是真实的、值得分享的。警告发布得很慢，甚至根本没有。因此，当大风暴来临时，公众常常措手不及。

