when you have too many categories in a scale or too many, say, colors on a map, it actually becomes harder and harder for people to interpret that because we can't... In our minds, it's very hard to deal with too many categories. Five is a very good, nice round number that the human mind can absorb and understand. And so I'll just say, you know, our point of view is that fours and fives are still catastrophic. What more is there than that? And nobody's been able to answer that. There's not another word beyond catastrophic that can categorize what that
当你等级中的分类太多，或者地图上的颜色太多时，人们解读起来实际上会变得越来越难，因为我们无法……在我们的脑海中，处理太多的分类是非常困难的。5 是一个非常好的、漂亮的整数，人脑可以吸收和理解。所以我会说，你知道，我们的观点是，4 级和 5 级仍然是灾难性的。除此之外还有什么？没有人能够给出答案。除了“灾难性”之外，没有别的词可以对这种破坏进行分类。

60
00:30:44.000 --> 00:31:15.000
damage would be. Totally understand that and totally understand why they're saying, let's not run off in this direction too quickly. Again, this is Richard Olson, who directs the International Hurricane Research Center. But I just go back to the fact that if we're seeing storms in the 180s or higher, I don't want to be playing catch up with nature. So Olson has been thinking about what kind of research the wall of wind can contribute to the debate.
完全理解这一点，也完全理解他们为什么会说，让我们不要太快朝着这个方向发展。再次说明，这是理查德·奥尔森，国际飓风研究中心的负责人。但我还是要回到这个事实：如果我们看到风速达到 180 英里甚至更高风力的风暴，我可不想跟在大自然后面疲于奔命。因此，奥尔森一直在思考风墙可以为这场辩论贡献什么样的研究。

