One that will go up to 200 miles per hour and have a big water tank to simulate storm surge and waves too. It'd be quite, quite large. Think of the wall of wind ten times the size probably. They want to simulate the whole hurricane, not just the wind part, in order to figure out how buildings and infrastructure might be able to withstand stronger storms. And that kind of facility would actually do something that goes beyond Saffir-Simpson, which points to what may be the scale's single biggest flaw.
这种风洞时速可达每小时200英里，还配有一个大型水箱，用来模拟风暴潮和巨浪。它会非常非常大。想象一下，风墙的尺寸可能是原来的十倍。他们想要模拟整个飓风，而不只是风的部分，以便弄清楚建筑物和基础设施如何能够承受更强的风暴。这种设施实际上可以做一些超越萨菲尔-辛普森等级的事情，这也指出了该等级可能存在的最大缺陷。

